1. Why static_cast was used (in simple terms)
Think of data types like different languages—for example, int (whole numbers) and float (decimal numbers) store numbers completely differently
inside the computer's memory.

If you just copy the raw 1s and 0s from a float directly into an int without translating them, you get total garbage. static_cast acts like a
translator: it looks at the actual math value of the float (say, 42.7), drops the decimal, and writes 42 in the proper int format.

It also acts as a safety guard when you compile your code. If you accidentally try to translate two types that have no logical relationship
(like turning a number into a pointer to a Bureaucrat), static_cast stops you with a compiler error before your program can ever run and crash.

2. Why impossible happens (in simple terms)
Every data type is like a box of a different size:

A char is a tiny box (only holds numbers from -128 to 127).

An int is a medium box (holds up to about 2.1 billion).

A double is a massive box (holds huge numbers and decimals).

impossible happens for two simple reasons:

The box is too small: If you have the number 500 in a double box and try to shove it into a tiny char box (which maxes out at 127), it won't
fit. Instead of letting the number overflow and turn into a corrupted value, we say it is impossible.

The concept doesn't exist: Decimal types like float and double have special labels for "Infinity" (inf) and "Not a Number" (nan). Whole-number
types like int and char simply don't have a way to write "Infinity"—there is no whole number for it, so converting it is impossible.

3. Why a non-instantiable (private constructor) class is useful
In C++, when you normally create a class, you use it to build objects that hold their own personal data (for example, two Bureaucrat objects
where one is named "Bob" with grade 5, and another is named "Alice" with grade 120).

Sometimes, however, you don't need objects that remember data—you just want a toolbox of helper functions grouped together under a clean name.

Making the constructor private prevents anyone from creating useless empty objects in memory, forcing them to just reach directly into the
toolbox using ClassName::function().


